<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rose of Lima</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1586">1586</a>–<a href="/years/1617">1617</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Saint Rose of Lima, Isabel Flores de Oliva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rose of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> was a Peruvian lay member of the Dominican order known for her severe asceticism, prayer and care for the poor and sick of Lima. Canonised by Pope Clement X in 1671, she was the first person born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to be declared a saint. She is the patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, of the Americas and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rose-of-lima.jpg" alt="Rose of Lima" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claudio Coello · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sta_Rosa_de_Lima_por_Claudio_Coello.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rose was born Isabel Flores de Oliva in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 20 April 1586, one of eleven children of Gaspar Flores, a harquebusier in the Spanish army, and María de Oliva y Herrera, a criolla native of Lima. Her later name came from an incident in infancy, when a servant claimed to have seen her face change into a rose. In 1597 she was confirmed by Archbishop Toribio de Mogrovejo, who was also later declared a saint, and formally took the name Rosa.</p><p>In emulation of Catherine of Siena she began as a girl to fast three times a week and to perform severe penances in secret. When her beauty was admired she cut off her hair and rubbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">peppers</a> on her face. She rejected all suitors, against the wishes of her family, and wanted to take a vow of virginity, which her parents opposed. Her father forbade her to become a nun, so she entered the Third Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-dominic">Saint Dominic</a> while living at home, and in her twentieth year took the habit of a tertiary and a vow of perpetual virginity.</p><p>She abstained permanently from meat, slept at most two hours a night, and wore a heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> crown with small spikes inside, in imitation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-thorns">Crown of Thorns</a>. She sold needlework, lace and flowers she grew to help her family and to care for the poor, and she brought sick and hungry people to her room. For eleven years she lived this way, with intervals of ecstasy, praying and doing penance in a small grotto she had built.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rose died on 24 August 1617, aged 31, after a long illness, and is said to have foretold the date. All the public authorities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> attended her funeral in the cathedral. She was beatified by Pope Clement IX on 10 May 1667 and canonised by Pope Clement X on 12 April 1671. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> holds that many miracles followed her death, among them that she had cured a leper and that Lima smelled of roses at the hour she died.</p><p>Her feast entered the General Roman Calendar in 1729 on 30 August, because 24 August is the feast of Saint Bartholomew; Pope Paul VI's revision of 1969 moved it to 23 August. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and some other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> countries 30 August remains a public holiday. Her skull, crowned with roses, is displayed in the basilica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, and many places in the New World are named Santa Rosa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rose of Lima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Catholic Church organised parishes, missions and confraternities, and Lima produced <b>Rose of Lima</b>, canonised in 1671 as the first saint of the Americas, and Martin de Porres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1606" aria-label="Lima on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2433 2613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rose of Lima"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2883 2883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2883 2883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1606">What was here in 1606? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
